## 4. Languages Used

### TypeScript

**Why chosen:** TypeScript was chosen as the sole language for the entire backend and bot. The application handles complex, nested data structures (Discord interaction payloads, multipart form uploads, encrypted token objects) where type safety catches bugs at compile time instead of runtime.

**Used in:** Everything under `src/` — all bot code, all API code, all shared utilities.

**Would another language be better?** Python with discord.py + FastAPI would be a reasonable alternative. However, since both discord.js and Express are JavaScript-first ecosystems, TypeScript is the natural fit without incurring any cross-language friction. Go would be faster for the HTTP layer but lacks a mature discord library.

| Pros | Cons |
|---|---|
| Compile-time type safety across the entire codebase | Requires build step (tsc) for production |
| Native to Node.js / npm ecosystem | TypeScript errors can be complex to diagnose |
| Shared types between bot and API (monorepo advantage) | `NodeNext` module resolution adds `.js` extension boilerplate |
| Large community, excellent discord.js types | Slower startup in dev (using tsx to skip compilation) |

### HTML5

**Why chosen:** The web portal is a single static page. No framework overhead was needed or appropriate.

**Used in:** `public/portal.html` — all UI structure, all form elements, the XHR upload logic.

| Pros | Cons |
|---|---|
| Zero dependencies | No component reusability |
| Served as static file — no build step | JavaScript is untyped in HTML `<script>` tags |
| Instant page load | Harder to maintain as complexity grows |

### CSS3

**Why chosen:** Vanilla CSS with custom properties (CSS variables) was chosen for the full design system. No TailwindCSS or CSS-in-JS.

**Used in:** `public/style.css` — the entire dark glassmorphism design system (1,000+ lines).

**Design techniques implemented:**
- CSS custom properties (design tokens for colors, spacing, radii, shadows)
- `backdrop-filter: blur()` for glassmorphism cards
- SVG `stroke-dashoffset` animation for the circular progress ring
- CSS `@keyframes` for floating logo, drifting background glows, animated success checkmark
- Responsive layout via `@media` queries

| Pros | Cons |
|---|---|
| No build tooling required | Verbose — no utility classes |
| Full control over every pixel | No automatic purging of unused rules |
| Modern CSS features are very powerful | Harder to maintain component-level styles |

